Here's a complete example of a C# MCP server that wraps a REST API using stdio transport:

---

## Tool Class — `ApiTools.cs`

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get a user profile by ID from the REST API.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUser(
        [Description("The unique user ID")] string userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching user {UserId}", userId);
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync($"/users/{userId}", cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Search users by name. Returns matching profiles.")]
    public async Task<string> SearchUsers(
        [Description("Name or partial name to search for")] string query,
        [Description("Max results to return (1-50)")] int limit = 10,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Searching users: {Query}", query);
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/users?q={Uri.EscapeDataString(query)}&limit={limit}", cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }
}
```

## Program.cs — stdio transport

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC protocol channel
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

// Register HttpClient with a base address for your API
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<ApiTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The MCP SDK integrates with the standard .NET DI container (`Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection`). Two injection styles:

| Style | How it works |
|-------|-------------|
| **Constructor injection** (non-static class) | The SDK creates your `[McpServerToolType]` class via DI, resolving constructor params like `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, etc. |
| **Method parameter injection** (static class) | DI services can appear directly as method parameters. The SDK resolves them — they never appear in the tool's JSON schema. |

**Key rule:** The SDK automatically hides injected services (`HttpClient`, `ILogger`, `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<T>`) from the LLM. Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed in the tool schema.

### To scaffold this project from scratch:

```bash
dotnet new mcpserver -n MyApiServer
cd MyApiServer
# Replace the generated files with the code above
dotnet build && dotnet run
```

The server will start, listen on stdin for JSON-RPC messages, and respond with tool listings and results when called by an MCP client (VS Code, GitHub Copilot, etc.).
